I have no problem with documentation; I'm the type of person who, 
ordinarily, does read instructions. Not sure what you mean by "ergonomic 
interfaces." My comment above is mostly a response to the blanket statement, 
"Read the Guidebook," applied to any question whose answer could be found 
therein.

My comments below may be more understandable if I relate my experience on 
first reading the Guidebook. I had happened upon NetHack as a part of the 
Debian package, and it defaulted to tiles when first opened; I didn't know 
until later that the ascii option existed. In the Guidebook, I read first 
the mock-legendary Introduction, which (iirc--I may be confusing this with 
the man file) in earlier versions said that the Amulet of Yendor was located 
"somewhere below the twentieth level of the dungeon," which is true but 
misleading--I recall Krysia's Krusader being confused on this point. After 
the description of the various roles and races, there is a description of 
what is happening on the screen; but since the description related to an 
interface far different than what I was seeing, it rapidly became confusing 
and irrelevant. Then there was the section on commands; although useful, 
there seem to be a bewildering number of them to a beginning player. The 
feeling I got was, "I'm never going to remember all this." At this point, 
I'm already skimming, not reading thoroughly. Therefore, I'm quite sure I 
missed much of the information located in the largely helpful sections 5-8. 
Section 9 begins to get into technical details (mixed in with option 
settings that do affect gameplay significantly). I'm quite sure it was a 
long, long time before I got any further than that.

> Not sure what you mean by "unreadable". On my part, as a non-native
> speaker, I find it very comprehensible and well structured, so that
> you quickly find everything you need.

Unreadable, in the sense of unable to be read straight through without one's 
eyes glazing over. (Just in case that's an idiom you're not familiar with, 
it means boredom resulting in loss of concentration and therefore loss of 
comprehension.) My feeling is that "you quickly find everything you need" 
only if you already know what you're looking for; many of the really useful 
things that cannot be found out by using the ? and / commands are buried in 
the text. A few examples:

- The magical powers of "Elbereth" are described under the E command, rather 
than in the sections describing monster behavior and combat. The effect is 
that if you don't already know about engraving Elbereth, you're unlikely to 
find it.
- Two-weapon combat is described under the x and #twoweapon commands, rather 
than in the section about combat.
- How to get a saddle off a steed is described under the #loot command; 
again, you have to know the command in order to find the command.
- Knowing how to bless items is alluded to, although not specifically 
described, in the "potions" section, but not in the section about blessed 
and cursed items.

> And it comes as a text file
> enabling you to grep/search for keywords. And it comes as a TeX file
> giving you a pretty printout. (And it comes for free. Thank's! :-)

Yes, you can search for keywords; however, if you simply go to what you 
think is the relevant section (such as monsters, combat, or blessed and 
cursed items) and don't find your answer there, you may reasonably assume 
(incorrectly) that the Guidebook doesn't address the question. If you *have* 
to do a search every time you're looking for something, that's evidence that 
the structure is deficient.

It has also been the case in my experience that the keyword I was looking 
for was hyphenated, and therefore was not returned when the document was 
searched. At the very least, the Guidebook should be changed into a simple 
text file without headers, footers, and hyphenations. Almost any text or 
word-processing program will give a person printing the document the option 
to add headers and footers, should they be desired.

> > If the 
> >>actual gameplay help and advice were separated from technical issues and 
> >>stuff dependent on the reader playing in ascii, it would be much improved.
> 
> There are two points mentioned, the ASCII part and the separation issue.
> 
> The ASCII base of the game makes it possible to describe it in a text
> file. To describe other GUI's you'd need to embed some picture formats
> and it would thus result in voluminous text document formats; *that*
> would, IMO, be a "monstrosity", as you call the current version.

I wouldn't suggest that at all; nor is it needed. With tiles, it's clear 
what a wall is, and you can tell the difference between a door and a 
spellbook. (I'm not, BTW, suggesting starting an ascii vs. tiles flamewar, 
which I know I'd lose anyway. I'm merely stating that tiles are, with regard 
to dungeon features, pretty self-explanatory, and if you come upon the game 
as I did, with tiles as the default, and then read the Guidebook, the first 
part of the Guidebook that you're likely to view as unhelpful is the early 
section on dungeon features in ascii.)
> 
> And since the guidebook is structured, you can always skip sections.
> Would it be such an advantage to separate the respective chapters into
> several (two or more) files? 

I wouldn't do that; but I would move the "dungeon features," "commands," and 
technical information into three separate appendices (within the same file), 
referenced from within the generally helpful sections now numbered 5 through 
8 (which would thereby be moved further toward the front of the document). 
In other words, the sections most likely to be skipped should be moved 
toward the end, where they can be referred to as needed; and the sections 
with helpful gameplay information be placed more up front, where they would 
be more likely to be noticed.

> (Maintaining *.mn, *.tex, and *.txt
> file versions for each book?) And there's also the *.html version
> (http://www.nethack.org/v343/Guidebook.html). Maintaining one text
> version and constructing the multitude of formats is superior, IMO.

One text version should be sufficient, period. People can format it to their 
liking, if they so choose. A good html version would be very helpful, if it 
hyperlinked keywords to other sections of the document where they would be 
explained; the present html version (iirc) simply provides a hyperlinked 
"index" to the various headings: only helpful if you already know where you 
want to go, and positively misleading if the information you're looking for 
isn't under the heading you expected it to be.
> 
> And if someone voluntarily contributes versions, extracted by topic
> from the Vanilla version, and enhanced by GUI pictures as needed, no
> one would be angry with him. :-)

If the DevTeam didn't pick it up, it would be largely useless.

